The ‘A vos soins’ association project is based on several key principles:
Access to healthcare for all, with the widespread introduction of direct billing (social security and supplementary health insurance);
All forms of care, whether or not they generate income, and all patients are accepted;
Any budget surpluses are reinvested in developing the health centre’s activities and in free preventative initiatives open to all.
The ‘A vos soins’ association also differs from other, more traditional organisations in its relationship with patients. Nurses take the time to explain the options available throughout the care pathway. Patients, regarded as active participants in their own healthcare, are represented on the association’s board of directors.
The team at the association A vos soins has designed and fitted out a former occupational health van, which is now equipped with a shower, a…

La Maison des Enfants Extraordinaires offers therapeutic workshops, holiday camps and private treatment services for children.
The association also supports the parents of these ‘extraordinary’ children through:
Advice and therapeutic guidance;
Parenting support workshops;
Training courses to help them better support their child;
Fun workshops designed to give parents a breather.
Finally, MEEX offers training courses for professionals who work with children with neurodevelopmental disorders on a daily basis (healthcare professionals, teachers, etc.).
Beyond geographical accessibility, the association aims to ensure that its services are accessible to all families, regardless of their budget. Consequently, whilst parents currently contribute 40 per cent of the cost of MEEX’s services, a ‘Coup de pouce’ scheme is available for families experiencing financial difficulties. This scheme,…

The association:
Implements more sustainable waste management through reduction at source and the recovery of food waste;
Supports urban agriculture by improving soil quality and reducing the use of chemical fertilisers through the use of compost;
Contributes to improving air quality by reducing greenhouse gas emissions through the use of bicycles;
Combats climate change by sequestering carbon in the soil through composting;
Promotes local employment and the creation of dedicated roles open to people on integration schemes;
Creates social spaces in the city centred around composting and gardening;
Changes the public’s perception of waste through awareness-raising initiatives.
